Pinned recurrent state cells - mm14, the precondition for a speculative
verify pass to reuse its graph at all.

Chain of evidence from the 2026-08-02 campaign: a verify pass costs ~45ms
against a 15.5ms graph-replayed single-token step; the gap is un-graphed
kernel launches; launches persist because ggml-cuda never captures a
batched graph; capture never happens because llama rebuilds the graph
every verify; the rebuild happens because llm_graph_result::can_reuse
fails - and on hybrid-SSM models it fails on exactly one condition,
llm_graph_input_rs::can_reuse's `head == mctx->get_head()`.

Why the head moves: enclave speculation verifies on a scratch branch
(seq_rm + seq_cp per round, the design hybrid models force since partial
rewind is impossible). Stock seq_cp gives the destination the SOURCE's
tail cell, and find_slot's "gather and re-order" step then swaps cells so
participating sequences sit contiguously - so the scratch sequence's
recurrent cell index, and with it the memory head, changes every round.

Change: pin each sequence's recurrent state cell to index == seq_id.
  - seq_cp gives the destination its OWN pinned cell and records the
    source in cell.src, which the graph's s_copy already honours as a
    copy-on-write (rather than sharing the source's tail). It falls back
    to the stock path when that index is owned by another sequence, so
    the invariant "a cell's seq_id set matches its tail" always holds.
  - find_slot prefers the sequence's pinned index when allocating a fresh
    cell, and skips the gather/re-order swap for pinned cells - but ONLY
    for a SINGLE-sequence ubatch (mm27, 2026-08-04).

mm27 concurrency fix. The two find_slot behaviours (prefer-pinned-index,
skip-reorder) were originally unconditional, and validated SINGLE-STREAM
only. Under real multi-user load they abort the tenant: the "update pos"
loop after the reorder unconditionally reads and re-tails cells[s+min]
(the contiguous slot), so skipping the pack for pinned cells that sit at
arbitrary indices re-tails the WRONG cell -> "find_slot: non-consecutive
token position" -> next round's GGML_ASSERT(cell.has_seq_id(seq_id))
kills the wasmtime process (users saw "the stream ended before the
response finished / instance likely restarted"). Gating both on
n_seqs == 1 is exact: with one sequence, min == that sequence's pinned
tail, so s+min already IS the pinned cell and nothing is skipped wrongly;
single-stream output is bit-identical to pre-mm27 (the added condition is
always true there). With several sequences the stock pack runs and the
layout is correct - graph reuse is lost to batch-shape churn under
concurrency anyway, so nothing of value is given up. seq_cp's pinning is
unchanged (it is per-copy and already guarded); the crash was purely the
find_slot reorder-skip. Reproduced + fixed locally: 4-way concurrent
hammer on the 9b aborts round 1 with the old unconditional skip, clean 8
rounds with the n_seqs==1 gate, pinning ON.

Validated on the real path (wasmtime + llm-chat + lookup drafting =
branch verify, qwen3.5-0.8b hybrid, temperature 0): output byte-identical
to stock, sha dea8944c8f5558f4 both ways. LLAMA_RS_PIN_CELLS=0 restores
stock behavior without a rebuild.
